#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92b54d77-fce2-53bf-aaad-4c7db8285162" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "fb897f41-dd8e-561f-be0e-02d2f9972203" :question/math-academy-id "e-5393" :question/problem "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=4\\sin\\theta$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "The polar equation\n\n$$\nr=2a\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\ndefines a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ centered at the point $(0,a)$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr=4\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nr=4\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\ngives a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\left|2\\right| = 2\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(0,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6a1f74e065f3508c92f8a438eb1830aeb4f877c355cd7779e86183a0da92183.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22b3da96-2573-513e-ae70-2b49510acd87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "9958848d-a3ec-599e-bbc4-44b3993bc707" :question/math-academy-id "e-5394" :question/problem "What is the polar equation of the curve shown below?\n\n![](images/0a/0ad44bfa452da482fff5f579f1be9569670f7ad8da347314bcb63f618eaf3d2a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & =\\end{vmatrix}-4.5 &= 4.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(0,-4.5)$.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 2a\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-4.5) \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&=-9\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ad4cf0e-4346-5277-9026-122b5ae58f25" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/id #uuid "7e0646a3-f4d2-5a29-ac05-519494a06177" :question/math-academy-id "e-5396" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=k\\cos\\theta$, where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $\\left(-\\dfrac72,\\dfrac72\\right)$. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A$. The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\frac{7}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(\\frac{7}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= π - \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\arctan \\mid - ((\\frac{7}{2}))/((\\frac{7}{2})) \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(r, \\theta\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= k\\cos θ \\\\\n\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} &= k\\cos (\\frac{3π}{4}) \\\\\n\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} &= k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\nk &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\nr=-7\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8aec18033a85129c44765d2670ebe9c931b0c0186c46b74f9b0ea0ae14028b76.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105669 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3504bc1-05d2-55c5-b862-84be2e2516f7" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "dd2bf9b1-9198-5040-9ef4-9924b4181108" :question/math-academy-id "e-7598" :question/problem "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "The polar equation\n\n$$\nr=2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\ndefines a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ centered at the point $(a,0)$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr=2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{2}{2} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nr=2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\ngives a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\left|1\\right| = 1\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(1,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bc5f763522aec68e97bef90126866fe6b2fe7246560d4dc9ddd2c71a5a524d88.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3504bc1-05d2-55c5-b862-84be2e2516f7" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95f16191-954d-5643-9248-20476e9329a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06c99a85fb7d6b56241d0f574424a47397698dc1bb0b9d15d6ba49637126fc8b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102307} {:answer/id #uuid "ac33cadd-3317-5a8b-bff1-ee122245f136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/1641aad508d38da90e1df72f98131faffb0cdbc19ee43abc946b47a86f800586.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102308} {:answer/id #uuid "bd310d16-0f42-527a-970e-972cbcb2e1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/05996c6895718404008ed930e4410d7f410f946568721b06736bd7915afa88ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102309} {:answer/id #uuid "d0579e02-5f8e-5de8-aad3-67d38a2e66cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc198a4464f8e0a9bc78907bf199acbce3bd4f61baf089739eed8497bfd0d22e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102310} {:answer/id #uuid "dda4921d-0d6c-577b-8e75-9b878d7aa0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer